**Theorem 5.4 (complete alternative on the recognized class).** For every
$(I,B)\in\mathcal C_{\rm img}$,

$$
\forall h\in\mathbb N\ J_B(h)
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\exists\tau\ \exists\mathbf p\in G_\tau.
\tag{NIS.18}
$$

A terminating mathematical procedure returns either a method producing
legal finite original witnesses to $J_B(h)$ for every supplied $h$, or a
finite $H$ with $\neg J_B(H)$. Every witness satisfies the entire original
input and its exact complete row. Contacts, finite barriers and functional
presentations are synthesized rather than assumed.

Proof. All formulas in (NIS.17) are Presburger by Proposition 4.3,
Theorem 4.4 and the owning symbolic negative projection. Presburger decision
therefore determines whether any $G_\tau$ is nonempty. Choose one fixed
$\mathbf p$ from a positive set before receiving any prefix depth.

Compute the canonical original coordinates and the common whole completed
source output $W$ through the exact forward and inverse reconstructions.
For a requested $h$, prescribe the depth-$h$ prefix of every canonical
infinite original residual; keep each finite original residual exactly fixed.
Choose one joint $\mathbf w$ in (NIS.17) with every divergent entry at least
$M_h=2h+2+\max(\{0\}\cup\{-c:\text{a divergent atom has offset }c\})$.
Then every divergent atomic occurrence has actual rank at least $2h+2$
and agrees through $h$ with $\Omega$.
At those same ranks, $\Psi_{r,\tau}$ supplies feasibility of the entire
negative list. [SLP, Lemma 2.1][SLP] produces one finite tuple with all
prescribed infinite-coordinate prefixes and all of that source's negatives
simultaneously. Its hypotheses apply to the original Cartesian chart
coordinates, not to constrained shifted states. Repeated names use one
filling. Different source copies can use different finite fillings, even when
their completed coordinates coincide.

Evaluate each original $\Theta_{r,b_r}$ with its actual ranks and filling.
Nonexpansion makes every resulting source agree through $h$ with the one
$W$. Both chart directions give its entire input, both reached guards for
every sign, exterior, reached set and full partition. Cross-source completed
equations need not hold as exact finite equations. Each finite original
satisfies its own signed predicate exactly. Presburger witness selection,
prefix computation and SLP construction terminate for the supplied $h$.

Conversely suppose all $J_B(h)$ hold. Lift actual finite source tuples through
the exact covers as themselves. A fixed chart tuple occurs at unbounded
depths. For each of finitely many original ranks, pass to a subsequence on
which it is constant or tends to infinity; retain all prior selections.
Compactness gives a further convergent residual tuple. Finite-mode atoms
remain fixed, divergent atoms tend to $\Omega$, and continuity plus increasing
common prefixes makes the entire completed source outputs equal.

The preliminary equivalences put this limit in one handled branch with fixed
finite auxiliary parameters. A finite atomic alternative uses its actual
limit rank; escape belongs to the separate $\Omega$ branch. Theorem 3.3 maps
this same original completed valuation to the functional system with all
its image filters. Choose the actual free-state saturation mode of this
solution. Theorem 4.2 proves canonical image admissibility for that mode.
It does not assert that the canonical solution equals the original limit.

Every canonically finite original coordinate nevertheless equals the original
limit coordinate, by Theorem 4.4. Isolation forces all such coordinates of
the finite witness sequence eventually to be these exact values at once.
Every divergent original rank exceeds any prescribed common $M$ sufficiently
late. The original finite witnesses still satisfy all their full signed
predicates and original rank policies, so they witness
$\Phi_\tau(\mathbf p,\mathbf w)$ at that $M$. Hence the one fixed
$\mathbf p$ belongs to $G_\tau$. No negative or partition was passed to a
completion point; only actual finite witnesses and finite isolation were used.

If all sets $G_\tau$ are empty, necessity proves failure of the all-depth
statement. Now query $J_B(0),J_B(1),\ldots$ using the existing
[NSF, Lemma 5.1 and Theorem 5.2][NSF] fixed-depth full-source decision:
one source per row, all its original constraints, and a single common
reply table. Every query terminates, and the proved failure ensures a first
finite NO. Thus exclusion search terminates. Without exhaustive handling
and the necessity proof, depth enumeration would not have this guarantee.
$\square$

